```python
@cp.fuse
def rmmmi_fused(n: int) -> int:
    return cp.sum((cp.arange(1, n) % 10) * 2 + 1)
```
would result in 6 kernels (`arange`, `%`, `*`, `+`, and 2-pass CUB reduction) being launched instead of 2 (for reduction-only). It turns out `arange` was not fusible, presumably because it allocates new buffer and `@fuse` cannot handle this? But we should be able to see through the fused ops and allocate the final buffer, right?

It'd be nice if this is doable, as it's a bit surprising to see no perf gain without/with `@fuse` for this case, but it's just a nice to have as we already figured out a workaround (by passing in an `arange`'d array from outside of the fusion context).","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cupy/cupy/issues/8689/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"timeline_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cupy/cupy/issues/8689/timeline","performed_via_github_app":null,"state_reason":null}}

